## Automated AI review

> **Community review:** Independent automated community review, unaffiliated with the Omarchy team, intended to help prepare PRs for their review.

**Outcome:** A right press on empty bar space cancels a widget drag; all six findings of the [earlier review](https://github.com/omacom/omarchy/pull/13140#issuecomment-5852462645) are resolved at `1624a877`.

### A right press on empty bar space cancels a widget drag

During a widget drag, a right press on empty bar space reaches `CenterGestureArea`; `onPressed` returns early with the event accepted. Qt gives it the grab and cancels the widget's `modulePointer`, so the widget is not dropped. **Impact:** the reorder is lost, so the [reply's](https://github.com/omacom/omarchy/pull/13140#issuecomment-5866112231) "ignored" holds only for the bar's own press and move; at the merge base the drag continued. Reproduced in a Qt 6.11.2 model, not the shell.

**Suggested change:** in that early return, reject the press (`mouse.accepted = false`) unless the gesture area itself holds the left button.

<details><summary>Reproducer (standalone Qt model)</summary>

Standalone Qt Quick model, not the running shell. `repro.qml` holds the bar's empty-space gesture area with `onPressed` copied as written from `CenterGestureArea` in `shell/plugins/bar/Bar.qml` at `1624a877` (comments dropped; a script compared the two blocks textually before the run). Over it sits one widget slot whose `MouseArea` takes only the left button, as `ModuleSlot`'s `modulePointer` does. `repro.cpp` drives it through `QTest`'s `QWindow` mouse functions, which keep the held-button state, so the right press arrives with the left button still held.

Run with Qt 6.11.2 (`qt6-base 6.11.2-3`, `qt6-declarative 6.11.2-1`), offscreen.

`repro.qml`:

```qml
import QtQuick

// The bar's empty-space gesture area (CenterGestureArea in
// shell/plugins/bar/Bar.qml at 1624a877, onPressed copied as written;
// openMenu stands in for a press on empty bar space) under one widget slot,
// whose modulePointer takes only the left button, as ModuleSlot's does.
Item {
  id: root
  width: 600
  height: 30
  property int widgetDragCancels: 0
  property int widgetDrops: 0

  MouseArea {
    id: gestureArea
    anchors.fill: parent
    acceptedButtons: Qt.LeftButton | Qt.RightButton
    property bool dragging: false
    property bool suppressClick: false
    property real pressedX: 0
    property real pressedY: 0
    property bool menuOpen: false
    function openMenu(x, y) { menuOpen = true; return true }

    onPressed: function(mouse) {
      if (mouse.button === Qt.RightButton) {
        if (dragging || (mouse.buttons & Qt.LeftButton)) return
        if (menuOpen) menuOpen = false
        else if (!openMenu(mouse.x, mouse.y)) mouse.accepted = false
        return
      }
      menuOpen = false
      dragging = false
      suppressClick = false
      pressedX = mouse.x
      pressedY = mouse.y
    }
  }

  // A widget slot's modulePointer: fills the slot, left button only.
  MouseArea {
    x: 200
    width: 60
    height: 30
    acceptedButtons: Qt.LeftButton
    property bool dragging: false
    onPositionChanged: dragging = true
    onReleased: if (dragging) root.widgetDrops++
    onCanceled: root.widgetDragCancels++
  }
}
```

`repro.cpp`:

```cpp
// Build: g++ -std=c++17 -fPIC repro.cpp -o repro $(pkg-config --cflags --libs Qt6Quick Qt6Test)
// Run:   QT_QPA_PLATFORM=offscreen QT_QUICK_BACKEND=software ./repro repro.qml
// QTest's QWindow mouse functions keep the held-button state, so the right
// press arrives with the left button still held, as from a real mouse.
#include <QGuiApplication>
#include <QQuickItem>
#include <QQuickView>
#include <QtTest/QTest>
#include <cstdio>

int main(int argc, char **argv)
{
  QGuiApplication app(argc, argv);
  QQuickView view(QUrl::fromLocalFile(QString::fromLocal8Bit(argv[1])));
  view.resize(600, 30);
  view.show();
  if (!QTest::qWaitForWindowExposed(&view)) return 1;
  QObject *root = view.rootObject();

  // Drag a widget left, over empty bar space, and press the right button
  // there before letting go of the left one.
  QTest::mousePress(&view, Qt::LeftButton, {}, QPoint(230, 15));
  QTest::mouseMove(&view, QPoint(225, 15));
  QTest::mouseMove(&view, QPoint(100, 15));
  QTest::mousePress(&view, Qt::RightButton, {}, QPoint(100, 15));
  QTest::mouseRelease(&view, Qt::RightButton, {}, QPoint(100, 15));
  QTest::mouseRelease(&view, Qt::LeftButton, {}, QPoint(100, 15));

  std::printf("widget drag cancelled: %d, widget dropped: %d\n",
              root->property("widgetDragCancels").toInt(),
              root->property("widgetDrops").toInt());
  return 0;
}
```

Actual output, `repro.qml` as above (head behaviour):

```text
widget drag cancelled: 1, widget dropped: 0
```

Actual output with the gesture area's `acceptedButtons: Qt.LeftButton` (as at the merge base, before the right-click menu):

```text
widget drag cancelled: 0, widget dropped: 1
```

Actual output with the suggested guard (the only change to `repro.qml`):

```qml
        if (dragging || (mouse.buttons & Qt.LeftButton)) {
          if (!(gestureArea.pressedButtons & Qt.LeftButton)) mouse.accepted = false
          return
        }
```

```text
widget drag cancelled: 0, widget dropped: 1
```

Why: the right press is delivered to the gesture area by hit-testing. `onPressed` returns with the event accepted, and Qt makes the accepting item the exclusive grabber of the mouse point (`QQuickDeliveryAgentPrivate::deliverMatchingPointsToItem`, `setExclusiveGrabber`), which cancels the previous grabber, the widget slot's `modulePointer`, so `onCanceled` runs (`Bar.qml` `modulePointer.onCanceled` calls `root.clearBarDrag()`). When the gesture area itself holds the left press (a bar move), the grabber does not change and nothing is cancelled.
</details>

<details><summary>Details</summary>

In a larger Qt model of `CenterGestureArea`, the guard shown in the reproducer leaves the bar-move and card cases unchanged. The regex at `bar-test.sh:47` would need updating.

**Verified:**
- `pills|floating toggle` with the real `omarchy-bar` and `omarchy-shell` against a `qs` stand-in: `no-bar`, a shell without the method, not ready and not running flip `shell.json` and exit 0; a timeout exits 1 and leaves it unchanged (`floating toggle` run for `ok`, `no-bar` and timeout).
- In the Qt model, a left press-and-hold followed by a right press keeps the card closed and finishes the move on the left release (at `97118bab` it opened the card and left the move unfinished). A second right press closes the card.
- Removing the left-button term from `onPressAndHold` now fails `bar-test.sh`; at `97118bab` the same mutation passed.
- `PanelGeometry.js` and the notification `barClearance` give the same values as the inline code they replace for a flush bar.

**Optional notes:**
- `toggle_in_shell` reports every other `omarchy-shell` failure, for example `Too many arguments provided` after a CLI/shell version mismatch, as "did not answer in time". The command still fails without touching `shell.json`; matching `*"is not responding"*` for the timeout message and using `the bar did not toggle: $result` otherwise would name the real cause.
- The `onPressed` comment says Qt cancels the move on a right press during a left press; on empty bar space the move actually carries on to the left release.

**Related open PRs:** #13569 adds the same `bar.floating` key and `omarchy bar floating` command with a different inset (one full `gaps_out`), and #13570 uses the layout `"group"` key to draw ovals, where this PR uses a `"group"` change to split pill runs.
</details>

